00:00:22.935 --> 00:00:23.440
Hola!

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.180
My name is Felipe Hoffa.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.100
You're interested in cool
ways to analyze data.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.710
Prepare to rewind.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.259
I chose this talk
because it highlights

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:34.900
some of the coolest new
features in BigQuery--

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.480
geography functions
and BigQuery ML.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.120
Yes.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.150
BigQuery now has
native functions

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.920
that understand geography.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.070
And now you can also use
BigQuery to create and execute

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.240
machine learning models
in the database--

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.110
in SQL, where your data lives.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.620
In this talk, we can see
how Bob and Daniel at Geotab

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.080
use these new BigQuery
features together

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.540
to predict how road
traffic in a city

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.940
will change under any
set of conditions.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:06.490
Geotab is a company that already
has more than one petabyte

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.310
of data stored in BigQuery,
with data points collected

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.640
from millions of vehicles
moving around the world.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.220
But before getting
into the big demo,

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.310
our BigQuery Product
Manager, Chad Jennings,

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.130
explains what is BigQuery
and the significance

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.370
of these amazing new features.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.350
BigQuery is our
serverless data warehouse

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.920
that you can use to store
and analyze all of your data,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.680
no matter what size.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.630
And all you need to know is SQL.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.520
Now we're adding native
geography functions

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.250
to BigQuery, so
companies like Geotab

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:43.990
can analyze the real-world data
in even more powerful ways.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.930
Let's say you have a point
in the world, or a line,

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.740
or a polygon, or an arbitrary
collection of things.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.710
BigQuery now has a new
geography data type that

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.360
can encapsulate any of this.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.660
And once you have
geography elements,

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.520
BigQuery can operate over them.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.520
You can ask for measures
like distance, length, area,

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.090
or transform these geographies
with unions and differences,

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.300
or find out if they
intersect, or are contained

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.970
between each other,
and even join tables

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.130
based on these predicates.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:16.970
Is the world a perfect sphere?

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:17.570
Nope.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.092
And BigQuery now
understands that.

00:02:20.092 --> 00:02:24.130
We even created a new tool to
easily visualize the results

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.390
of your SQL queries--

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:28.190
the BigQuery Geo Viz tool.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:31.840
And then, with BigQuery ML,
you can create machine learning

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.790
models where your data lives.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.560
Your data analysts
and scientists

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.480
can create an
experimental wheel,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.940
finding the best
features within your data

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.600
to create predictions
inside the data warehouse

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.780
and without sweat.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.340
Train, evaluate, predict!

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.396
